[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the MonoBehaviour Lifecycle Execution Order in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every script in Unity that inherits from MonoBehaviour goes through a predefined sequence of events from the moment it is loaded until it is destroyed. Mastering this execution order is crucial for writing predictable, bug-free gameplay systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Initialization Phase"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The initialization phase is responsible for setting up variables, references, and initial states before any physics or rendering updates occur."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Awake(): Called when the script instance is being loaded. This is always called before any Start functions and even if the script component is disabled. Use this for initializing variables or references between scripts."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OnEnable(): Called when the object becomes enabled and active. This is also called immediately after Awake if the object is active upon creation."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Start(): Called only if the script component is enabled, right before the first frame update. Unlike Awake, Start is guaranteed to run after all Awake methods across all scripts have finished."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Simulation and Update Phase"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once initialization is complete, Unity enters the runtime loop. This phase handles physics calculations, frame-by-frame logic, and coroutines."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"FixedUpdate(): Called at a fixed time interval, independent of frame rate. Use this for all Rigidbody physics calculations."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Update(): Called once per frame. This is the main function for regular game logic, input handling, and non-physics movement."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LateUpdate(): Called once per frame, after Update has finished executing. Ideal for camera tracking or following objects that moved in Update."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Cleanup and Destruction Phase"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a GameObject is destroyed, disabled, or the application quits, Unity triggers cleanup methods to free resources."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OnDisable(): Called when the behaviour becomes disabled or when the GameObject is being deactivated."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OnDestroy(): Calleded after all frame updates are complete, just before the object is permanently destroyed."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Implementation"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following code template demonstrates the most commonly used methods in a typical MonoBehaviour script:"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class LifecycleDemo : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Awake()
    {
        Debug.Log("Awake called: Initializing internal references.");
    }

    void Start()
    {
        Debug.Log("Start called: Game logic begins.");
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // Handle player input and movement here
    }

    void OnDestroy()
    {
        Debug.Log("OnDestroy called: Cleaning up resources.");
    }
}"}]
